course: religion-ethics
lesson: 58
title: When a grandparent dies
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: When a grandparent dies, their body has stopped working and they will not wake up or come back; it is very sad, and it is never a child's fault, whatever was said or done before.
explain: Picture Ellie at breakfast: in the night her mum got a phone call, and now she says, «Grandad died in hospital in his sleep. He wasn't in pain.» Ellie asks, «Dead dead? Like proper dead?» and Mum says yes, and holds her.
explain: Grown-ups often use soft words: «we lost Nana», «she passed away», «she's away now» in Scotland, «gone to be with the Lord»; these all mean the person has died, and it is fine to ask a grown-up to say it plainly.
explain: Grief comes in waves: a child may play football as usual, feel relief that the pain is over, forget for a whole day at the beach and then feel guilty, and all of this is normal and does not mean the love has gone.
explain: Ellie's church teaches that Grandad is now with God and that Christians will meet again in the resurrection; other faiths have their own hopes, some people believe death is the end, and all agree that love and memories stay.
fact: When a person dies, their body stops working; they will not wake up or come back.
fact: «Lost», «passed away», «passed on» and «she's away» are gentle ways of saying someone has died; clear words help children most, because a child told «gone to sleep» may become scared of sleep or keep waiting for the person to wake.
fact: Words cannot make someone die; a death from illness or old age is never a child's fault.
fact: Children grieve in different ways: some talk, some cry, some play as usual; grief can come later, in waves.
fact: Some families visit the body at the funeral home to say goodbye; the person looks peaceful but is not breathing and feels cool; children may go if they wish, with a grown-up.
fact: Orthodox and Catholic Christians, and many cultures, often sit with the body and pray before the funeral.
fact: When someone cannot travel to a funeral, they can say goodbye by lighting a candle, writing a letter, looking at photos or joining by video.
fact: In the Ukrainian Orthodox Church, a panakhyda is a prayer service for someone who has died.
fact: When someone has suffered for a long time, people can feel relief that the pain is over and great sadness at once.
fact: A will is a document in which a person says who should get their things after they die.
fact: Forgetting about the person for a while is how minds heal; it does not mean the love has gone.
fact: Funerals often include music the person loved, such as a football song, alongside hymns and readings.
exercise: Ellie asks, «Is Grandad dead dead?» What is the honest answer? | Yes, his body stopped working and he won't come back | No, he is only in a very deep sleep and will wake when he is better | He will wake up again once the funeral is over and done | No, he has gone abroad for a while and will ring soon
exercise: Tommy's mum says, «We lost Nana last night.» What does she mean? | Nana has died | Nana is missing in the shops | Nana lost a game of cards | Nana has moved house
exercise: Ava plays football at school as if nothing happened after her grandma died. What is true? | Playing can be a way of coping; grief can come later | It means she never really loved her grandma at all | She must be made to sit down and talk about it today | She will never feel sad about her grandma at all, ever
exercise: Harvey called Grandad boring last month. Did that make him die? | No, words cannot make someone die; it was illness and age | Yes, rude words said to someone can really stop their heart | Partly, so Harvey must go and say sorry to the doctor now | Yes, but only if Grandad heard it and was hurt by it
exercise: Holly wants to see her gran's body to say goodbye. What is wise? | She can go if she wants, prepared first and with a grown-up | Children are never allowed to see a body, at any age at all | She must go, whether she wants to or not, as a duty | She should go in alone, so that nobody sees her upset
exercise: What does a person look like at the funeral home? | Peaceful, but not breathing, and cool to touch | Exactly as if they will sit up and wake any minute now | Frightening, and covered in blood and bruises all over | Like a grey statue carved out of cold, hard stone
exercise: Jack's uncle in Glasgow says, «She's away now, wee man.» What does he mean? | Jack's granny has died | Jack's granny is on holiday | Jack's granny moved to England | Jack's granny went out to the shops
exercise: Mia's grandad died in Ukraine and they can't travel to the funeral. How can she say goodbye? | Light a candle, write a letter, look at photos or join by video | There is no way at all to say goodbye from far away | Only by flying there for the funeral, whatever it costs | By pretending at home that he is still alive and well
exercise: What is a panakhyda? | A Ukrainian Orthodox prayer service for someone who died | A Ukrainian cake for birthdays | A kind of Ukrainian folk dance | A letter sent to the dead by post
exercise: Liam says «Good, she's not in pain any more» about his nan, then feels bad. Is he bad? | No, relief and sadness can come together | Yes, a good grandson should never feel any relief at all | Yes, feeling relief means he secretly wanted his nan to die | No, but only because he never really loved his nan anyway
exercise: Freddie asks who gets Grandad's watch. What is true? | Families share things out, often by a will; asking gently is fine | Children may never have anything at all of Grandad's to keep | The vicar decides who gets everything of Grandad's once the funeral is over | Everything he owned must be buried with Grandad in the grave
exercise: What is a will? | A document saying who gets a person's things after death | A promise to visit the grave every week | A prayer said at the funeral | The name for a grandad's watch
exercise: Jodie forgot her grandma for a whole day at the beach. Is she a bad person? | No, forgetting for a while is how minds heal | Yes, and she must never go to the beach again to make up | Yes, it shows she never loved her grandma in the first place | No, because her grandma is not really dead, only far away
exercise: At Grandpa Joe's funeral they play «You'll Never Walk Alone». Why a football song? | Funerals often include music the person loved | Football songs must be played at funerals | The family wanted everyone to stand and cheer for Liverpool | The vicar only knows football songs, so he chose one himself
exercise: What do Christians at Ellie's church believe about Grandad? | He is with God, and they will meet again in the resurrection | He has turned into an angel with wings who watches over them | He will come back to earth as a new baby some time next year | Nothing at all happens to him; he simply stops, and that is all
test: A Gen Z cousin texts, «grandad is dead dead?? like fr?» («fr» means «for real».) What should the grown-ups say? | Yes, sadly; he has died and won't come back | No, he's only resting in hospital for a while | He'll be back home again after the funeral | It's not fr, it's just a joke the grown-ups made
test: Tommy, confused, says, «If we lost Nana, let's go find her!» What should his mum say? | «I'm sorry, love, I mean Nana died. She isn't lost.» | «Yes, let's look for her in town» | «She's lost, but she'll find her own way home» | «Don't worry, the police are searching»
test: Ava snaps, «I don't wanna talk about it, OK?» at break. What can her friends do? | Just be there, and tell her they're around if she wants | Keep on asking her again and again, every break, until she talks | Ignore her for a week until she feels better herself | Tell the whole class that she is sad, so they know
test: Harvey sobs, «I said he was boring. Did that kill him?» What is the truthful answer? | No, words can't make someone die; Grandad knew you loved him | Yes, rude words like that can really stop a heart | Maybe, so you should go on feeling bad about it for ever and ever | Only if you said it to him more than once, to his face
test: Jack, from Glasgow, asks, «Whit dae ye mean away? Away where?» («Whit dae ye» means «what do you».) What is the wise answer? | His uncle means granny has died; it's fine to ask him to say it plainly | Granny has gone away on a long bus trip to see her sister | Granny is away at the Kirk and will be home for her tea later | Granny will be back home after the weekend, once she has had a good long rest
test: What would happen if grown-ups only ever said «she's gone to sleep» about a death? | A child might be scared of sleep or wait for her to wake | Children would understand death perfectly | Nobody would ever feel sad | The person would really wake up
test: Mia moans, «We can't even say bye. That's so unfair.» Which is the best answer? | It is hard, and there is more than one way to say goodbye | Then she has no way at all to say goodbye to him now | It doesn't really matter much, because he lived so very far away | She can only say bye properly if she goes to Ukraine
test: Liam's mate from Bristol says, «Gert sad about your nan, but she's not poorly no more, eh?» («Gert» means «very»; «poorly» means «ill».) Liam feels glad and sad at once. Is that okay? | Yes, relief and sadness can go together | No, feeling glad means he didn't really love her | No, he must choose just one feeling and stick to it | Yes, but only once the funeral is properly over
test: Freddie blurts, «Can I have his watch?» before the funeral. What's a gentler way? | Ask later and say, «I'd like something to remember him by» | Grab it from the drawer first, before anyone else does | Ask for Grandad's car and his telly as well, while he's at it | Never ask for anything at all, not even something small
test: A Gen Alpha girl says, «Forgetting your nan at the beach is so delulu, you're a bad person.» («Delulu» means «deluded».) What is true? | Forgetting for a while is how minds heal; having fun isn't betrayal | She is right; you must think about your nan every single minute of every day | Forgetting her for a day means the love has gone for good | Going to the beach so soon is disrespectful to the dead
test: A Liverpool grandad's family plays «You'll Never Walk Alone». His grandson asks why a footy song. («Footy» means «football».) Best answer? | It's the song he loved, so it helps tell the story of his life | Football songs must be played at every funeral in Liverpool | The family just wants to annoy the vicar and the church choir on purpose | It's the only song the church owns, so they had to use it
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the expression «we lost Nana last night»? | A euphemism signifying that Nana has died | A report that Nana has become physically lost | An admission that the family was defeated in a game | A statement that Nana has relocated
exam: Identify the principal cause of the death of an elderly grandparent from illness. | The illness and advanced age of the deceased, and not the conduct of any child | A discourteous remark made by a grandchild shortly before the death | The grandchild's failure to visit the grandparent sufficiently often in the final years | The grandchild's having enjoyed a holiday during the final illness
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the instrument known as a will. | A document specifying the disposition of a person's property after death | A prayer pronounced at the committal of the deceased | A promise by the family to visit the grave | A certificate confirming the cause of death
exam: Ascertain the means by which a bereaved family unable to attend a distant funeral may nonetheless take leave of the deceased. | By the lighting of a candle, the writing of a letter or participation by video | By no means whatsoever, since distance renders any farewell impossible | Solely by attending the funeral in person, at whatever cost | Solely by the purchase of flowers to be delivered abroad
